 \maketitle
 
 \begin{abstract}
-We evaluate a budget-aware two-phase greedy index tuner and the Wii call-skipping mechanism (Wang et al., SIGMOD 2024) on TPC-H at scale factor 10, using PostgreSQL 18.7 with default planner settings. Over four independent statistics draws, Wii finds indexes that improve the workload cost by 32.7\% on average (32.2--33.3\%) using 429--437 what-if calls, against 33.9\% with about 3\,040 calls for the same tuner without Wii (96\% of the quality with 14\% of the calls); at tight call budgets (500--1000) it is 3.3--7.7 points better than the budgeted baseline in every draw. The alpha parameter trades calls for quality smoothly, random skipping is far worse than Wii's bound-based skipping, and the cost model satisfies Wii's monotonicity and submodularity assumptions in more than 98\% of tested cases. Our implementation of Esc (Wang et al., PVLDB 2025), an early-stopping checker on top of the same tuner, saves 17--28\% of the calls at $K=20$ without measurable loss. Under storage budgets ($K=100$) it needs a storage-aware bound to stop at all; with it, Esc saves 5--28\% of the calls (more at larger budgets) in 14 of 16 budgets at a loss of at most 0.2 points, and it adds nothing on top of Wii, because Wii's skipped calls leave its upper bound loose. Against the eight algorithms of the original study at a 10~GB budget (three repetitions each), the two-phase tuner reaches 67.3\% relative cost with 2\,828 calls (CoPhy 66.3\% with 23\,066, Relaxation and DTA 67.6\%), and with Wii 68.3\% with 430 calls. The Wii results are means over four statistics draws and the Esc results over two or three; the assumption and gap experiments use one draw; costs are optimizer estimates.
+We evaluate a budget-aware two-phase greedy index tuner and the Wii call-skipping mechanism (Wang et al., SIGMOD 2024) on TPC-H at scale factor 10, using PostgreSQL 18.7 with default planner settings. Over four independent statistics draws, Wii finds indexes that improve the workload cost by 32.7\% on average (32.2--33.3\%) using 429--437 what-if calls, against 33.9\% with about 3\,040 calls for the same tuner without Wii (96\% of the quality with 14\% of the calls); at tight call budgets (500--1000) it is 3.3--7.7 points better than the budgeted baseline in every draw. The alpha parameter trades calls for quality smoothly, random skipping is far worse than Wii's bound-based skipping, and the cost model satisfies Wii's monotonicity and submodularity assumptions in more than 98\% of tested cases. Our implementation of Esc (Wang et al., PVLDB 2025), an early-stopping checker on top of the same tuner, saves 17--28\% of the calls at $K=20$ without measurable loss. Under storage budgets ($K=100$) it needs a storage-aware bound to stop at all; with it, Esc saves 5--28\% of the calls (more at larger budgets) in 14 of 16 budgets at a loss of at most 0.2 points, and it adds nothing on top of Wii, because Wii's skipped calls leave its upper bound loose. Against the eight algorithms of the original study at a 10~GB budget (three repetitions each), the two-phase tuner reaches 67.3\% relative cost with 2\,828 calls (CoPhy 66.3\% with 23\,066, Relaxation and DTA 67.6\%), and with Wii 68.3\% with 430 calls. The Wii results are means over four statistics draws and the Esc results over two or three; the assumption and gap experiments use one draw; costs are optimizer estimates. When the indexes selected at a 5\,GB budget are built and the 19 queries executed (one budget, one selection per configuration), only the plain two-phase greedy improves the runtime (by 35\%); the other five configurations are 3--40\% slower than with no indexes, although all six are estimated to improve the workload by 26--29\%.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \section{Setup}
@@ -162,19 +162,44 @@ Dexter (10\%) & \multicolumn{3}{c}{70.7} & 0 & 2.4\\
 
 \textbf{Esc} changes the cost of the plain tuner by at most 0.2 points while saving up to 28\% of its calls (previous section), which puts it at 2\,184 calls at 10\,GB. Caveats: the tuner uses index width 2 and the original algorithms the study's own configuration; calls are counted by different code paths (the platform's cost-request counter for the original eight, the oracle's calls for ours); the original eight and our variants come from different runs; all costs are optimizer estimates.
 
+\section{Real execution}
+Everything above uses optimizer estimates for hypothetical indexes. For the third objective of the proposal we built the indexes selected at a 5\,000\,MB budget for real in the SF10 database and ran the 19 queries (\texttt{scripts/execute\_selected\_indexes.py}; runtime per query is the median of the timed repetitions, the analysis output reports three; a query is stopped after 5 minutes and counted as 5 minutes). The workload without indexes takes 669.9\,s. Indexes are those chosen by the first repetition of each configuration.
+
+\begin{table}[h]\centering\small
+\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}
+\hline
+Configuration & Indexes & Estimated & Real & Queries $>10\%$ slower\\
+\hline
+Extend & 6 & 28.7\% & $-7.7$\% & 10\\
+Relaxation & 7 & 28.8\% & $-3.3$\% & 9\\
+Two-phase greedy & 7 & 26.5\% & $+35.1$\% & 7\\
+Two-phase greedy + Wii & 3 & 26.4\% & $-19.4$\% & 9\\
+Two-phase greedy + Esc & 6 & 26.5\% & $-40.0$\% & 11\\
+Two-phase greedy + Wii + Esc & 3 & 26.4\% & $-38.3$\% & 9\\
+\hline
+\end{tabular}
+\caption{Improvement of the workload runtime over no indexes (positive is faster), estimated against measured, at 5\,GB.}
+\end{table}
+
+\begin{figure}[h]\centering\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig_execution.png}\caption{Estimated against measured improvement; the dashed line is agreement.}\end{figure}
+
+The estimates do not predict the measured result. All six configurations are estimated to improve the workload by 26--29\%, but only the plain two-phase greedy is faster in practice; the other five make the workload slower. Two-phase greedy and two-phase greedy + Esc have the same estimated improvement (26.5\%) and measured improvements 75 points apart, with index sets of 7 and 6 indexes. Queries 9 and 10 are more than 10\% slower in all six configurations, and several others (1, 4, 7, 12) in most of them. The differences between methods in the estimated tables (a few points) are much smaller than the gap between estimate and execution, so those tables do not show which method chooses better indexes in practice.
+
+What this does not show: that Wii or Esc choose worse indexes in general. It is one budget, one selection per configuration, and 19 queries; Esc returns the configuration of its simulated greedy, built from derived costs, which can differ from the plain tuner's; timeouts are counted at 5 minutes, which hides the size of the slowdown of the slowest queries. We have not examined which indexes cause the slowdowns, nor tested possible causes (for example statistics after the indexes are built, cache state or planner settings), so we give no explanation.
+
 \section{Discussion and limitations}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item \textbf{Statistics draws.} Table statistics come from a random sample, and different \texttt{ANALYZE} runs move the no-index cost by about 0.1\% and the improvement of a run by about 1 point (Wii 32.2--33.3\%, unlimited baseline 33.4--34.6\% over four draws). Absolute improvements should not be read to better than about 1 point; differences between methods on the same draw, and the ordering of the methods, were stable. The assumption and gap experiments (Sections 2.3 and 2.4) use a single draw. The comparison with the original algorithms (Section~4) uses three repetitions on both sides.
 \item \textbf{Planner settings.} A teammate's run with \texttt{max\_parallel\_workers\_per\_gather}=8 gave a lower cost scale (no-index cost $\approx$34.95M) and, as reported to us, the same qualitative patterns; we did not verify those runs and report only the default-settings results here.
-\item \textbf{Estimated, not executed, costs.} As in the original platform, costs are optimizer estimates for hypothetical indexes; queries were not executed in the experiments of this report. Running the selected indexes and timing the queries (the third objective of the proposal) is done by \texttt{scripts/execute\_selected\_indexes.py}; its results are not part of this report yet.
+\item \textbf{Estimated costs.} Except for the section on real execution, costs are optimizer estimates for hypothetical indexes. The real execution covers one budget (5\,GB) and one selection per configuration, and it shows that the estimates do not predict the measured result there; the estimated quality in the other sections should be read with that in mind.
 \item \textbf{Comparison with the paper.} The paper evaluates on other DBMS and workloads, with a different cost scale, so absolute improvements are not comparable; our results agree qualitatively with its motivation (many what-if calls are redundant; skipping them by bounds keeps most of the quality; budget reallocation helps most when the budget is tight). We did not reproduce its numbers.
 \item \textbf{One workload.} Only TPC-H at SF10 so far; TPC-DS is configured but not run.
 \end{itemize}
 
 \section{Status and next steps}
-\textbf{Done:} Wii on TPC-H SF10 with the alpha, random-skipping, gap and assumption experiments (four statistics draws); Esc-B with its clamped variant, the storage fix and the storage-aware bound; the comparison with the eight original algorithms under storage budgets, three repetitions.
+\textbf{Done:} Wii on TPC-H SF10 with the alpha, random-skipping, gap and assumption experiments (four statistics draws); Esc-B with its clamped variant, the storage fix and the storage-aware bound; the comparison with the eight original algorithms under storage budgets, three repetitions; real execution of the selected indexes of six configurations at one budget (5\,GB).
 
-\textbf{Still open:} (1) Real execution of the selected indexes (objective 3). (2) The ablation of the storage-aware bound on TPC-H. (3) A sweep of small call budgets (about 50--400) so that the budget limits Wii, and Esc under a call budget. (4) Repeat the assumption and gap experiments over several draws. (5) Esc-I and TPC-DS.
+\textbf{Still open:} (1) Real execution at more budgets and with several selections per configuration, and finding the indexes behind the slowdowns. (2) The ablation of the storage-aware bound on TPC-H. (3) A sweep of small call budgets (about 50--400) so that the budget limits Wii, and Esc under a call budget. (4) Repeat the assumption and gap experiments over several draws. (5) Esc-I and TPC-DS.
 
 \section*{Reproducibility}
 Repository \texttt{RameezWahid/magic-mirror-revisited}, \texttt{main} (two-phase greedy and Wii: \texttt{selection/budget\_aware.py}, \texttt{selection/algorithms/two\_phase\_greedy\_algorithm.py}, \texttt{scripts/wii\_experiments.py}). Esc: branch \texttt{esc-arijit} (\texttt{selection/esc.py}, \texttt{scripts/esc\_summary.py}, \texttt{scripts/compare\_algorithms.py}, \texttt{tests/test\_esc.py}). Storage-limited runs: \texttt{configs/tpch\_sf10\_new\_algos.json} through \texttt{scripts/run\_repetitions.sh} (three repetitions), then \texttt{scripts/analyze\_results.py}. Command: \texttt{wii\_experiments.py configs/tpch\_sf10\_load.json <out> assumptions gaps wii alpha random unlimited}, and \texttt{... <out> esc esc\_clamp} for Esc; the baseline numbers come from the platform's \texttt{RESULTS.txt}; run data in \texttt{runs\_rep1.jsonl} and \texttt{assumptions.json}. Three repetitions gave identical results except one coverage run (164 or 165 calls, cost difference 10 out of $2.9\cdot10^7$), likely from floating-point summation order.
